  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe key insight is to use dynamic programming to construct h incrementally, optimizing the maximum overlap at each step while respecting constraints. Unlike gradient descent, this method systematically explores the solution space and can handle constraints more directly. The algorithm builds h sequentially, making locally optimal choices that balance immediate overlap reduction and future implications. This structured approach may escape poor local optima and find a better global solution.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nWe model the problem as:\nmin_{h \u2208 [0,1]^n} max_k (h \u22c6 (1 - h))[k] * dx\nsubject to:\n- h[i] \u2208 [0, 1] for all i\n- sum(h) = n_points / 2\n\nDefine the dynamic programming state as the current partial h array and the maximum overlap up to each possible shift. The state transition considers adding each possible h[i] \u2208 {0, 1} (simplified from [0,1] for efficiency) and updating the maximum overlap for affected shifts.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na) Initialize with initial_h_values or a structured waveform\nb) Set up DP:\n   i) state = (current_h, max_overlaps)\n   ii) for each position i, try h[i] \u2208 {0, 1}\n   iii) compute resulting max_overlaps for each shift\n   iv) choose h[i] that minimizes the new max overlap\nc) After each step, project h to feasible space\nd) Periodically perturb h and restart DP\ne) Track best solution with lowest C\u2085\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Start with binary h (0/1) for faster exploration\n- Use quantized values for h (e.g., 0, 0.5, 1)\n- Adjust DP lookahead depth\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nThe DP approach is O(n^2), feasible for n=100. Runtime optimizations include early termination on maximum overlap and lookahead pruning. Memory is manageable with state compression.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nFinal h is repaired to exactly meet constraints, and C\u2085 is re-evaluated. All steps ensure feasibility.",
